const pause=`# Source writers are already drained and fenced.
# SOURCE_URL and DESTINATION_URL name the rehearsed databases.
# Destination is empty; roles, ownership and grants are handled separately.
pg_dump "$SOURCE_URL" -Fc --no-owner --no-acl -f orders.dump
pg_restore --dbname="$DESTINATION_URL" \\
  --single-transaction --exit-on-error orders.dump
# Keep destination writers closed until reconciliation and acceptance pass.`;
const stream=`-- Source: schema is already deployed at the destination.
CREATE PUBLICATION move_pub FOR TABLE orders, items, move_boundary;

-- Destination: empty tables; a separate PostgreSQL cluster.
-- The local worksheet uses its own loopback port and temporary drill role.
CREATE SUBSCRIPTION move_sub
  CONNECTION 'host=127.0.0.1 port=SOURCE_PORT user=drill dbname=online'
  PUBLICATION move_pub
  WITH (copy_data=true, streaming=off, synchronous_commit=on);
-- Submit CREATE SUBSCRIPTION outside BEGIN when it creates its slot.`;
const readiness=`-- Destination: compare this set with the explicit publication manifest.
SELECT srrelid::regclass AS relation, srsubstate
FROM pg_subscription_rel
WHERE srsubid=(SELECT oid FROM pg_subscription WHERE subname='move_sub');
-- All THREE expected relations must be present and 'r' (ready).

-- Source: ONLY after business writers are drained and fenced.
INSERT INTO move_boundary(token) VALUES ('online-final');
-- Autocommit completes before this is accepted as the final marker.

-- Destination: fresh query, after all tables have become ready.
SELECT token FROM move_boundary WHERE token='online-final';
-- Require the row, then final reconciliation and unreplicated-state checks.`;
const sequence=`-- Source, after fencing every allocator; fixture increment/cache are both 1.
SELECT last_value, is_called FROM orders_id_seq;
-- Observed final state: 5000 | true.

-- Destination, while application writes remain closed:
SELECT setval('orders_id_seq', 5000, true);
-- Next nextval() is 5001. Use YOUR recorded source state, not this literal.
-- Check sequence definition and all other sequences separately.`;
const run=`# Save the worksheet as rehearse.py in a disposable working directory.
# Python 3 standard library; installed PostgreSQL 18 server/client tools.
# Run as an ordinary OS user, with permission to start local processes.
python3 rehearse.py --pg-bin /path/to/postgresql-18/bin \\
  --evidence ./move-evidence.json`;
---

<p>Moving a database transfers the right to accept changes. Copying its rows is only part of that transfer. If Mina pays for order 101 while its earlier pending state is being copied, the destination must receive the payment too. If the source can accept another order after traffic moves, there are two places claiming to hold the shop’s current facts.</p>
<p>We will move one order database between PostgreSQL servers. Orders have item lines, unique references and generated identifiers; accepted orders must survive the move. The <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/#keep-old-and-new-applications-compatible">parent’s application-compatibility method</a> still applies. Here the additional problem is to connect the copied state to every intervening change, then establish one write authority.</p>
<p>The concrete rehearsal used native PostgreSQL <strong>18.6</strong> and its matching <code>pg_dump</code>, <code>pg_restore</code> and built-in logical replication. It exercised a complete paused move and a small copy-with-capture move. The figures are explanatory models; their order numbers and planning times are illustrative. Neither the figures nor the tiny local rehearsal establish production capacity or an acceptable service interruption.</p>
<nav aria-label="In this article" class="article-contents"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="choose-the-simplest-move-that-fits-the-write-pause">Choose the simplest move that fits the write pause</h2>
<p>Start with a service contract: which databases, tables and dependent facts move; how long writes may be unavailable; whether reads may continue or become stale; and how much acknowledged work may be lost. For this shop the loss allowance is zero. Name the person who can stop the attempt and the evidence they require before reopening orders.</p>
<p>A <strong>write pause</strong> stops new changes while a stable source is copied and checked. Rehearse the whole interval: admission stop, draining, fencing, export, transfer, loading, indexes and constraints, validation, refreshed connections and usable application service. Include the failed-validation response and a reserve. Destination preparation that can happen safely beforehand need not occupy the pause.</p>
<p>Figure 1 starts with a 15-minute allowance and a 12-minute paused procedure. Compare the five-minute allowance. The numbers are invented so the decision stays visible.</p>
<MoveFigure scene="budget" number={1}/>
<p>When the measured complete pause fits, choose that route. Keep destination writes closed, fence the source, make the export, load a clean destination, reconcile, test the application and deliberately reopen it there. A failure before destination writes leaves the source as the possible abort target after revalidation. There is no live change stream to operate.</p>
<Code code={pause} lang="sh" themes={themes}/>
<p>The worksheet restored 2,000 orders and 2,000 item lines, compared their exact values and marker, then created order 2101 through the destination application role. Its full dump carried the sequence state. The quoted options omit ownership and grants deliberately: the worksheet creates its own roles and supplies explicit grants. A real move must preserve or deliberately replace those contracts. The single restore transaction is a bounded small-fixture choice; it prevents accepting a partially committed restore but can be unsuitable for a large load. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pgrestore.html">The restore contract</a> specifies transaction and parallelism restrictions.</p>
<p>If the allowed pause is shorter than the rehearsed path, copying while writes continue can move most transfer work outside it. <strong>Change capture</strong> retains and applies committed changes after the starting copy. It earns its complexity only if the final fence, catch-up, reconciliation and admission fit the allowance. If applying changes cannot outrun their arrival, a fast initial copy merely leaves a growing backlog.</p>

<h2 id="prove-that-the-destination-preserves-the-workload-s-meaning">Prove that the destination preserves the workload’s meaning</h2>
<p>Follow actual shop operations before treating the destination as interchangeable: create an order, allocate its identifier, add item lines, pay once, cancel and list a customer’s recent orders. Record the result types and comparison rules those operations depend on. Test the application release that will actually connect, including concurrent attempts and its recovery of an uncertain request. <a href="/review/relational-next/applications/">Application integration</a> owns that request protocol.</p>
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Meaning to preserve in the order database move"><table><caption>The move’s compatibility manifest</caption><thead><tr><th>Workload dependency</th><th>Evidence at the proposed destination</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Amounts, statuses and relationships</td><td>Column types and precision, null rules, checks, unique references and order/item foreign keys preserve the accepted facts.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Reference comparison and ordering</td><td>Encoding, collation provider/version and comparison behaviour meet the query and uniqueness contract. Test representative strings, not only ASCII fixture references.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Time and generated values</td><td>Timestamp types and connection time zones preserve the intended instant and reporting day; identity defaults, sequences, generated columns and trigger effects are accounted for.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Objects beyond table rows</td><td>Required schemas, views, functions, extensions and their installed binaries, large objects and external dependencies have a supported transfer or reconstruction path.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Application access</td><td>Real roles, grants, row-security policies, authentication, TLS, network access, connection settings and representative queries work through new connections.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
<p>Our fixture keeps both servers at 18.6, UTF-8, <code>C</code> collation and UTC, with integer minor-unit totals, ordinary tables and no extensions, generated columns, custom triggers or large objects. Those choices bound what it tests. An engine conversion or changed collation needs its own representative semantic checks inside the move; successful row loading cannot prove equivalent comparisons. PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/collation.html">collation documentation</a> explains why rules and provider versions belong in the manifest.</p>
<p>Separate object deployment from row transfer. A full <code>pg_dump</code> exports one database’s supported definitions and values, but cluster roles and tablespaces need a separate inventory, for example a reviewed <code>pg_dumpall --globals-only</code> capture. Configuration, extension packages and secrets need their own provisioned destination. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pgdump.html">pg_dump</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/app-pg-dumpall.html">pg_dumpall</a> define those scopes. For logical replication we deploy compatible schema first and copy rows separately.</p>
<p>Freeze schema changes for this bounded move. If a change is unavoidable, stop the attempt until its order across source, subscriber and application is explicitly rehearsed. <a href="/review/relational-next/schema-changes/">Schema changes</a> owns the locks and physical deployment; <a href="/review/relational-next/data-changes/">data jobs</a> owns any guarded transformation. Diagnose slow destination queries through <a href="/review/relational-next/query-plans/">query plans</a>, keeping their workload and capacity acceptance in this move’s evidence.</p>

<h2 id="join-the-starting-copy-to-every-later-change">Join the starting copy to every later change</h2>
<p>A <strong>consistent snapshot</strong> supplies a declared visible state for copying. It is insufficient when later commits are allowed. The capture mechanism must connect its copy boundary to the changes it retains, covering inserts, updates and deletes with no omitted interval. It must also define overlap, ordering, transaction visibility and restart. “Start streaming when export finishes” leaves a gap.</p>
<p>Figure 2 copies pending orders 101 and 102, then changes their history. Compare starting capture too late. The fenced branch shows why a complete pause needs no stream join.</p>
<MoveFigure scene="join" number={2}/>
<p>For the concrete online route, use PostgreSQL’s built-in initial synchronisation rather than hand-joining a dump to an arbitrary WAL position. It copies each table using a synchronisation worker and catches that table up before handing it to normal apply. Changes follow source transaction order. A table visible during initial copying does not establish that the complete order database is ready; require the entire declared relation set ready before using its joint state. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-architecture.html">The architecture contract</a> describes this join and transient copy-worker restart.</p>
<Code code={stream} lang="sql" themes={themes}/>
<p>The worksheet uses one publication, one subscription, no filters or column lists, and all three tables: orders, items and the marker table. <code>copy_data=true</code> includes existing rows. It deliberately chooses <code>streaming=off</code> for a simpler committed-transaction path and <code>synchronous_commit=on</code> for durable subscriber apply in this drill. These are explicit choices, rather than the PostgreSQL 18 defaults. The source is not configured to wait synchronously for this subscriber on ordinary commits. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-createsubscription.html">CREATE SUBSCRIPTION</a> defines the options and creation restrictions.</p>
<p>Check eligibility table by table. Our primary keys identify updates and deletes. A table without an appropriate <strong>replica identity</strong> needs a supported identity choice before those operations can be published; <code>REPLICA IDENTITY FULL</code> has type and lookup-cost restrictions. Check partitions, row filters and publication operation choices against the real scope rather than assuming every table change is present. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-publication.html">Publication and replica identity</a> define this contract.</p>
<p>Ordinary PostgreSQL 18 logical replication does not carry DDL, sequence state or large objects. Its apply path normally runs with replication trigger semantics, so it is not equivalent to replaying application requests and their ordinary triggers. Inventory any enabled replica triggers and other downstream consumers, and ensure no copied outbox or restored worker dispatches an external effect twice. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-restrictions.html">The restrictions</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-architecture.html">apply semantics</a> define the transfer; <a href="/review/relational-next/background-work/">background work</a> owns external-effect recovery.</p>
<p>Provision source logical WAL, sender and slot capacity, subscriber workers and permitted replication identities in advance. Our fresh clusters use local trust and a drill superuser to test mechanisms; that does not validate production authentication or least-privilege deployment. Check <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-config.html">configuration</a> and <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-security.html">security</a> against the actual host’s supported contract.</p>
<p>Retained history is a storage obligation. Track the slot’s required history and validity, source free space, copy impact, backlog and catch-up rate with an owner and stop threshold. If required history expires, a table is omitted or an unsupported change occurs, keep destination writes closed. Resume only from a documented still-valid state; otherwise rebuild from a fresh supported copy boundary. Do not skip a failed transaction merely to make lag fall. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/#keep-the-stream-able-to-catch-up">the topology article’s retention method</a> for the underlying operating signals.</p>

<h2 id="rehearse-the-destination-and-reconcile-the-copied-state">Rehearse the destination and reconcile the copied state</h2>
<p>Run the actual procedure on representative volume and sustained workload, with the real destination identity, network path and tool versions. Measure copy, apply, final pause, validation and usable application restart separately. Include long transactions, large transactions, interruptions, permission failures and a validation failure. Record the tolerated limits, sample interval, owner and abort action. A warm loopback copy of a few thousand rows answers mechanism questions, not a service-volume acceptance question.</p>
<p><a href={worksheetUrl} download="rehearse.py">Download the disposable cutover worksheet</a>. It needs Python 3 and installed PostgreSQL 18 server and client tools, with no additional Python or JavaScript dependencies. It creates two new temporary clusters on unused loopback ports, uses only invented orders and contacts no existing database. Run it as an ordinary OS user:</p>
<Code code={run} lang="sh" themes={themes}/>
<p>The worksheet refuses to overwrite the evidence filename. Commands have deadlines, SQL has an eight-second statement limit and a two-second lock-wait limit, and catch-up waits expire after 12 seconds. These are drill limits. It retains exact commands, SQL, outputs, comparisons, errors, elapsed intervals and server logs, then stops both clusters and removes their temporary directories. Our run confirmed both stopped and the directory removed.</p>
<p>Reconcile only comparable state. For the pause route, source fencing makes the exported state stable. For the online route, first prove final apply after fencing; then keep both sides stable during the comparison. Before that point, independent live snapshots can legitimately disagree. A source count at 09:01 and a destination count at 09:02 do not describe one boundary.</p>
<p>Figure 3 starts with equal counts but the wrong status. Compare a wrong identity, then a matching copy. Watch which check each variation survives.</p>
<MoveFigure scene="reconcile" number={3}/>
<p>Check both directions: every in-scope source key is present, and every destination key is expected. Compare required values in explicit key ranges, recording coverage and exceptions. For this shop also verify item relationships and that each order’s total equals its accepted item calculation, with the actual rules for currency, discounts and rounding. A whole-table count and sum can both agree while two customers’ orders are wrong.</p>
<p>The native worksheet compares exact ordered rows of all three tables after the final marker. It injected total 999 into destination order 103: counts still agreed, while value comparison failed. After correction, exact comparison and the fixture rule <code>total = sum(quantity * unit_price)</code> passed. The fixture has one currency and no discounts. At service scale, a tested range comparison or digest can locate differences, but digest equality adds collision and canonicalisation assumptions; inspect discrepancies and independently test domain meaning.</p>
<p>Readiness also requires usable constraints, required indexes, statistics, generated identifiers and application-role behaviour. The drill checked a newly generated order and rejected a negative total through that role. Test the real queries, connection pool, representative concurrency, latency and error budget separately. Reuse <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/#prove-readiness-before-returning-traffic">restoration’s readiness evidence</a> when a restore supplies the starting copy; a verified backup alone does not open this move’s gate.</p>

<h2 id="fence-writers-and-prove-the-final-boundary-has-arrived">Fence writers and prove the final boundary has arrived</h2>
<p><strong>Fencing</strong> is enforced exclusion of source writes. Before starting, inventory request servers, old pools, retry queues, background workers, delayed tasks, schedulers, imports, administrators and any privileged or security-definer write path. Decide how each loses write authority and how that exclusion survives reconnects, restarts and automated controllers. Changing DNS or the normal request route does not close existing sessions.</p>
<p>Figure 4 follows the gates in order. At each gate, compare an old pool, a missing marker or an untransferred sequence. The model leaves later checks inspectable; the actual procedure stops at its first failed gate.</p>
<MoveFigure scene="cutover" number={4}/>
<ol>
 <li><strong>Stop admission.</strong> Application owner pauses every write producer, records outstanding operation identities and starts the declared interruption clock.</li>
 <li><strong>Drain or resolve work.</strong> Let bounded transactions finish, or cancel and discover their durable outcomes. Account for prepared transactions if the deployment permits them. A dropped commit reply does not prove abortion; use <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">request recovery</a>.</li>
 <li><strong>Enforce the source fence.</strong> Database/infrastructure owner excludes every inventoried writer, retires lingering sessions and verifies no write-capable producer remains. A failure here aborts before destination admission.</li>
 <li><strong>Record the final committed boundary.</strong> Migration owner records the mechanism’s scoped final marker or supported apply boundary, after all business writes finish.</li>
 <li><strong>Wait for final apply.</strong> Prove every expected table ready and the final boundary applied at the destination, within the pause budget. Missing evidence closes the gate.</li>
 <li><strong>Reconcile and prepare omitted state.</strong> Keep application writes closed; compare final facts, transfer identifiers and check schema, privileges and destination readiness. Acceptance owner signs the result before the traffic owner proceeds.</li>
</ol>
<p>The drill demonstrated why <code>ALTER ROLE move_app NOLOGIN</code> alone is incomplete: a previously connected transaction still inserted <code>old-pool</code>. It then combined NOLOGIN with revoking the fixture role’s table-write and sequence privileges and terminating its sessions. One terminated in-flight transaction left no committed order; a fresh source connection was rejected. That proves exclusion of this non-owner laboratory role. It does not fence superusers, inherited privileges, alternate credentials or hidden write functions in a real deployment. Use its supported infrastructure and database controls, with an explicit administrative freeze, to establish the complete fence.</p>
<Code code={readiness} lang="sql" themes={themes}/>
<p>Our marker protocol is deliberately narrow: all scoped changes and the marker use one unfiltered subscription; all three tables have reached normal replication; business writers remain fenced; no prepared work can commit later; and marker M commits after their final transactions. With this drill’s serial committed-transaction apply, a fresh destination read of M establishes that preceding scoped transactions have applied. It is stronger evidence than an idle “zero lag” display. It says nothing about unpublished tables, external effects or sequences. The worksheet explicitly verifies the expected three-relation set using <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/catalog-pg-subscription-rel.html">pg_subscription_rel</a>.</p>
<p>To rehearse interruption, it disabled the subscription and waited until its apply worker stopped before committing the marker. The marker was absent at the destination, so admission remained closed. Re-enabling the subscription delivered it; exact reconciliation then passed. The source history remained retained throughout this short interruption. A production attempt needs an explicit catch-up deadline and abort path when its allowed pause expires. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-altersubscription.html">ALTER SUBSCRIPTION</a> defines enable/disable; <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logical-replication-monitoring.html">subscription monitoring</a> identifies workers.</p>
<p>Sequence state supplies a separate gate. The source’s last allocated value was 5000, with gaps from allocations that did not produce retained rows. At the subscriber the sequence still read <code>101 | false</code>; its first generated insert collided with existing order 101, error <code>23505</code>. Replicated identity-column values had arrived, while the allocator had not.</p>
<Code code={sequence} lang="sql" themes={themes}/>
<p>Copying the final <code>last_value</code> and <code>is_called</code> is sufficient for this fixture’s identical, ascending, increment-one, cache-one sequence after all allocators stop. A real policy must cover every sequence’s definition, cached reservations, cycles, descending increments, externally allocated identifiers and destination allocations made during testing. <code>max(id)</code> alone cannot account for reserved values or those other schemes. <code>setval</code> changes are not undone by transaction rollback; keep allocators excluded and verify the resulting state. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/functions-sequence.html">Sequence functions</a> define that behaviour.</p>

<h2 id="admit-destination-traffic-with-a-defined-way-to-stop">Admit destination traffic with a defined way to stop</h2>
<p>Once every gate passes, stop or detach the now-complete forward apply path according to the tested procedure and confirm it is quiescent before allowing independent destination changes. Keep source business writers fenced. Change application connection configuration, retire affected sessions and refresh old pools. Verify new connections reach the intended database under the real application identity; <a href="/review/relational-next/connections/">connection lifetimes</a> owns those client mechanics.</p>
<p>Admit a controlled order workflow, then bounded traffic while watching successful operations, required read-after-write results, constraints, permissions, latency, errors and capacity. Define the admitted workload, observation interval and limit before the attempt. The operator must be able to pause admission when any acceptance check fails. Do not silently reopen jobs merely because the HTTP path passed; their effects and permissions need acceptance too.</p>
<p>Figure 5 starts after destination order 103 commits. Compare a failure before any destination commit. The two return procedures have different data obligations.</p>
<MoveFigure scene="return" number={5}/>
<p><strong>Before destination writes</strong>, an abort can preserve the original history: keep the destination closed and fenced, verify it accepted no new work, revalidate source state and connections, then deliberately restore source admission. Proving “no new work” includes jobs and administrator activity, not merely the first failed application request.</p>
<p><strong>After destination writes</strong>, the source is a stale copy. Returning requires verified reverse synchronisation or another controlled move that preserves the destination’s new history and relationships. Decide its supported scope, conflicts, fencing and time budget before cutover. If no such return is feasible, record a destination repair and recovery path, with its attainable interruption, before accepting the move. Two independent application writes to both databases do not supply a correctness protocol.</p>
<p>The worksheet disabled forward apply after its marker and final checks, transferred sequence state and admitted order 5001 at the destination. A fresh application read found it paid with total 100; the source lacked it. That is direct evidence that routing back would conceal accepted work. The drill did not implement reverse replication, prove a real application pool or benchmark bounded production admission.</p>

<h2 id="close-the-move-with-evidence">Close the move with evidence</h2>
<p>Record the source and destination identities and versions, transfer scope, compatibility results, final committed boundary, relation readiness, reconciliation coverage and exceptions, identifier state, application acceptance and the current write authority. Include measured intervals, workload impact, failed gates, unresolved operation outcomes and the actual return decision. The next operator should be able to explain why a new order may be accepted here.</p>
<p>Keep the source fenced for the agreed decision window. Hand destination monitoring, replication protection and a tested recoverable copy to operations; the move is incomplete if accepting orders destroys the service’s recovery promise. Use <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/">topology operation</a> for replica duties and <a href="/review/relational-next/backup-and-restore/">backup and restoration</a> for recoverable state. A retained source frozen at cutover is not a backup of later destination orders.</p>
<p>Give temporary capture, slots, accounts, connection secrets, exported files and comparison data an owner and removal gate. When forward capture is no longer needed, remove it deliberately and verify source slots have gone; an inactive slot can still retain WAL. The drill dropped its subscription, confirmed its source slot absent, then stopped and removed only its own two clusters. <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/sql-dropsubscription.html">DROP SUBSCRIPTION</a> also defines failure when the publisher cannot be reached; disassociating a slot does not itself remove that remote resource.</p>
<p>Retire the old database only after acceptance, the decision window, recovery handoff and any required evidence retention permit it. Remove its obsolete access and automation before destroying its stored facts. <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/#retire-old-data-and-prove-the-change-is-finished">The parent’s lifecycle method</a> owns that retirement, including downstream copies and restored data. The move closes when the authority, preserved history and remaining recovery choices are explicit.</p>

<h2 id="sources">Sources and further reading</h2>
<p>Checked on 20 October 2026. Native observations use PostgreSQL 18.6 on one Apple-silicon laptop, two fresh clusters and the downloadable Python worksheet. Each route starts with 2,000 orders and item lines. The run exercised paused export/restore, initial logical synchronisation, intervening inserts/updates/deletes, a surviving old connection, termination of uncommitted work, disabled apply and catch-up, failed value and sequence gates, and fresh-role destination writes. It measured no production capacity, remote failure domain, hosted migration service, reverse transfer, extension conversion or real application pool. All five figures are deterministic models.</p>
